\mt1 Psalms

\c 1

\s1 The Two Paths

\d \f + \fr 1:0 \fk KONTEXT \fq No superscription \ft Psalm 1 has no Hebrew superscription — one of only two exceptions in Book I (Psalms 1–41; the other is Psalm 2). This omission is editorial, not accidental. Together with Psalm 2 (also unsuperscribed), Psalm 1 forms a two-part prologue to the entire Psalter: a wisdom gate (Ps 1) and a royal gate (Ps 2). Both end with *ashrei* acclamations — Psalm 1 opens with "How happy is the one" and Psalm 2 closes with a matching declaration (2:12). The Psalter begins not with a prayer, a hymn, or a petition but with a wisdom observation: *before you pray, position yourself*. In Jewish morning liturgy (*Shacharit*, *Pesukei d'Zimra*) and in the Benedictine weekly office (Prime on Monday), Psalm 1 functions as the weekly threshold — the door you walk through before everything else.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 1 How happy — the one who\f + \fr 1:1 \fk KULTUR \fq follow, linger, sit \ft The three verbs trace a progression, not three simultaneous acts. Rashi (1040–1105) reads them as three stages of moral habituation: temptation tried, then adopted, then entrenched. Basil of Caesarea (fourth century) sees the same arc. Goldingay: *plans* (ideas you follow) → *way* (a path you stay on) → *company* (a group you belong to). The person who merely walks alongside the wicked is not yet lost; the one who sits among the cynics has made their world home. — The verse is a deliberate reversal of Deuteronomy 6:7, the Shema instruction: "You shall speak of these words when you *sit* in your house and when you *walk* on your way." The three postures the Shema commands for Torah engagement are here repurposed negatively — for the wicked's company. Anyone who recognizes that echo hears a quiet question: *Where is your Shema?*\f*
\q1 doesn't follow the wicked's plans,
\q1 doesn't linger where sinners walk,
\q1 doesn't sit with the cynics.
\b
\q1
\v 2 Their \add one\add* desire: the LORD's instruction\f + \fr 1:2a \fk TEXT \fq the LORD's instruction \ft The Hebrew *torah* (*torat YHWH*) does not mean "law." Torah (from *yarah*, "to instruct, to direct") means instruction, teaching, guidance — the full body of divine wisdom: stories, commands, promises, histories, Genesis through Deuteronomy. Translating it as "law" imports the Reformation-era antithesis between law and grace — a framework the Hebrew text does not know and does not need. — The word *chephets* ("desire") has an explicitly affective register in Hebrew. In Song of Songs 7:11, the same word describes erotic longing: "His desire is for me." This psalm is not describing an obligation. It is describing someone who cannot get enough of God's teaching — the way a person falls in love with a book, a language, an idea, until it becomes part of how they think. No imperative verb appears in the entire psalm: this is portrait, not prescription.\f* —
\q1 they murmur it day and night.\f + \fr 1:2b \fk KULTUR \fq murmur \ft The Hebrew verb *hagah* describes a deeply embodied, voiced activity — not silent reflection. In Isaiah 31:4 it is the growl of a lion; in Isaiah 38:14 it is the mourning of a dove. The person who *hagah*-s over Torah is moving their lips, making a low rhythmic sound, letting the text pass through the body. In the ancient world, there was no such thing as silent reading — reading was always oral, even if only whispered. Hossfeld and Zenger describe *hagah* as "reciting semi-audibly." The biblical scholar Danilo Verde (2024) argues that this exact practice stabilized the post-exilic Jewish community when it had lost temple, king, and territory: you carry the stream *within you* by murmuring it. — The second *torah* in this verse reads *torato* in Hebrew — "his instruction" (no longer "the LORD's instruction"). Rashi observes: when someone engages Torah deeply enough, it becomes *their own* — it shifts from God's possession into the practitioner's life. This is not plagiarism. It is formation.\f*
\b
\q1
\v 3 Like a tree transplanted\f + \fr 1:3a \fk TEXT \fq transplanted \ft The Hebrew *shatul* does not simply mean "planted." It means specifically *transplanted* — taken out of one soil and placed into another. This nuance is theologically decisive: the tree did not naturally grow there. Someone moved it. Craigie (WBC): "transplanted — moved by intentional action into better soil." The psalm is not claiming that Torah practitioners naturally flourish. It is claiming that the person beside the water *was relocated there* — by a God who knows where the good soil is. The difference is between self-improvement and grace. — The Egyptian Instruction of Amenemope (ca. 1300–1075 BCE), the closest ancient Near Eastern parallel to this psalm, uses the same tree-beside-water image. But in Amenemope, the wisdom is available to any cultivated person. Psalm 1 narrows the claim: it is specifically the LORD's instruction that constitutes the water source. The tree was elsewhere. It was brought here.\f*\f + \fr 1:3a \fk TEXT \fq tree transplanted beside streams \ft How do we know we have what the ancient psalmist wrote? The textual record for Psalm 1 is unusually clean. The Hebrew Masoretic text and the Greek Septuagint (third century BCE) agree on the entire psalm with one minor expansion (LXX adds "from the face of the earth" in v.4). No Dead Sea Scroll fragment of Psalm 1 has ever been found — and that absence is itself revealing: Psalm 1 predates the Qumran community's library (ca. 200 BCE forward). The text was already so settled that it did not need to be copied for sectarian use. Peter Flint's 1997 study of the Psalms scrolls confirms: where DSS witnesses do exist for psalms, they show remarkable agreement with the later Masoretic tradition. — A separate question: is Psalm 1 derivative of Egyptian wisdom literature, or is it doing something distinct? The honest answer is *both*. The verbal proximity to the Instruction of Amenemope is real and acknowledged across the scholarly spectrum (Hossfeld–Zenger, Craigie, Goldingay). Israel was not writing in isolation. But Psalm 1 transforms what it inherits: in Amenemope, wisdom is a quality available to any cultivated person; in Psalm 1, the water source is specifically the LORD's instruction, and the tree's relocation is a covenantal act. The psalm enters an ancient Near Eastern conversation and changes the subject.\f* beside streams of water\f + \fr 1:3b \fk KULTUR \fq streams of water \ft *Palge mayim* in Hebrew describes natural or artificial irrigation channels — the infrastructure that in the semi-arid landscape of ancient Palestine made the difference between life and death. A tree beside such a channel is not a pastoral idyll. It is a sign of intentional care. The image resonates on multiple levels: (1) the Eden memory (Genesis 2:10–14: four streams flowing from the garden); (2) Zion theology (Psalm 46:4: "A river whose streams make glad the city of God"); (3) eschatological prophecy (Ezekiel 47:12: trees beside the Temple stream that always bear fruit, whose leaves never wither — identical imagery). The reader who hears this psalm hears not "nature is beautiful" but *you know which water source gives you life — and that source is me*.\f* —
\q1 it bears fruit when the time is right,
\q1 its leaf stays green,
\q1 and everything it does flourishes.\f + \fr 1:3c \fk UNGLAUBLICH \fq flourishes \ft The Hebrew *yatsliach* (flourishes, thrives) has a reception history that, in English-speaking evangelicalism, became the source of systematic harm. The King James Version translates "whatsoever he doeth shall prosper." In the American prosperity gospel, this became a formula: murmur Scripture enough and you will become wealthy. J. Clinton McCann and Nancy deClaisse-Walford (NICOT) have made clear: *yatsliach* is not an economic term but an ecological one. It describes the thriving of a living thing — growth, fruit, vitality. The tree does not "prosper" on a balance sheet. It *lives*, because its roots have water. — Three clarifications that matter: (1) Walter Brueggemann warns that Psalm 1 must always be read as the *first* word of the Psalter, never the last. The laments that immediately follow (Psalms 3–7 are all laments) are the Psalter's own correction: the claim of Psalm 1 is immediately and harshly tested by the reality of those who suffer. (2) Anyone who uses this verse to diagnose why someone is suffering — as if insufficient Torah-murmuring explains poverty, illness, or grief — reverses the text's meaning. This is a hope-claim, not a performance review. (3) The subject of *yatsliach* is grammatically ambiguous: it could be the tree or the person. Robert Alter notes the ambiguity is probably intentional — it "helps the tenor and vehicle of the simile marry."\f*
\b
\q1
\v 4 Not so the wicked.\f + \fr 1:4 \fk TEXT \fq chaff \ft The Hebrew *motz* is the winnowed husk — the light, insubstantial shell separated from grain during harvest and blown away by wind. The image assumes an agricultural world: utter weightlessness, no substance, no staying power. Everything the tree represented (roots, fruit, green leaf, endurance) is negated in a single word. The wind does not struggle to move chaff. It is already gone.\f*
\q1 Chaff. The wind takes it.
\b
\q1
\v 5 When it all comes due, they won't stand —\f + \fr 1:5 \fk KONTEXT \fq when it all comes due \ft The Hebrew *ba-mishpat* ("in the judgment") holds two readings open simultaneously: (1) the real community assembly — the council of elders that adjudicated everyday disputes in Israelite life; and (2) an eschatological divine reckoning. Hossfeld and Zenger favor the eschatological reading, since Psalm 1 forms an integrated prologue with Psalm 2, which has an explicitly eschatological horizon. Both readings can stand. — The word *tzaddikim* ("the just") appears here for the first time in the psalm — only in verse 5, after the wicked and sinners have been named repeatedly since verse 1. This delay is deliberate (NICOT): the positive counterweight is withheld until the negative has been fully explored.\f*\f + \fr 1:5 \fk LEBEN \fq two paths \ft The two-paths framework of this psalm — the just flourish, the wicked dissolve — carries a temptation that has struck repeatedly in the history of its use: reading it as a diagnostic tool against those who suffer. If the just flourish, then the person who is suffering must somehow be on the wrong path. This reading is the exact reversal of what the liberation theologian Elsa Tamez (*Bible of the Oppressed*, 1982) demonstrates: in the Psalter, *resha'im* ("the wicked") overwhelmingly designates the economically powerful, the exploiters — not the religiously deviant. The "just" are the poor, the displaced, those the system has written off. The psalm says to those the world calls chaff: *No. The wicked are the chaff. You are the transplanted tree.* — Psalm 1 belongs at the beginning of a journey, not its end. Psalms 3, 22, and 88 — the Psalter's own objections — show that this confident claim is immediately and harshly tested. The Psalter holds Psalm 1 *and* Psalm 22. It does not resolve the tension.\f*
\q1 not among the just.
\b
\q1
\v 6 The LORD watches over the way of the just.\f + \fr 1:6 \fk TEXT \fq watches over \ft The Hebrew *yodea'* ("knows") describes intimate, relational recognition — the same root *yada'* that in Genesis 4:1 describes sexual intimacy. God "knows" the way of the just the way you know someone *from the inside* — not as observer but as companion. English "watches over" captures the protective quality; the Hebrew adds a layer of tenderness that "watches" alone cannot fully carry. — NICOT identifies a decisive grammatical asymmetry in this verse: the just are the *object* of divine knowing (God knows their way — they are known and sustained). The wicked are the *subject* of their own dissolution (their way *comes to nothing* — they author it themselves). The psalm distributes divine care and human agency unevenly, and in the most hopeful direction. — Jerome (fourth century) makes an often-overlooked observation about the final clause: the verse does not say that *the wicked* come to nothing, but that their *way* does. "Not the wicked themselves will perish, but while they repent, wickedness vanishes." This is not absolution — it is an opening for change. — The first word of the psalm begins with the Hebrew letter *aleph* (\fq אַשְׁרֵי\fq*) and the last word ends with *tav* (\fq תֹּאבֵד\fq*): A to Z. Hossfeld and Zenger read this implicit frame as a completeness marker — the psalm offers life-instruction from beginning to end, from the first letter to the last.\f*
\q1 The way of the wicked comes to nothing.
